    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Absolutely, let's delve into the nitty-gritty of these delay metrics, as precise as a goblin's treasure map.\n\nSignalized intersections, during peak hours, can have an average control delay per vehicle ranging from 10 to 30 seconds. This is akin to goblins waiting for their turn to enter a treasure cave, with each goblin experiencing a delay that can vary based on the number of goblins and the size of the cave. The saturation level, or the point at which the intersection becomes as clogged as a goblin's pantry, typically occurs when the arrival rate of vehicles exceeds the capacity of the intersection, leading to queues that can extend for blocks.\n\nModern roundabouts, on the other hand, tend to have a much lower average control delay per vehicle, often between 2 to 5 seconds. This is because vehicles are continuously circulating, much like goblins on a merry-go-round, with each goblin (or vehicle) experiencing a shorter wait time. The saturation level for roundabouts is generally higher than for signalized intersections, as they can handle a greater volume of traffic before becoming congested. This is due to the reduced need for stopping and starting, which is a common cause of delays at signalized intersections.",
      "source": "target",
